producer_report.summary:
Read-ahead and the stale-reading re-check are in the parallel drain path (`drain_concurrent`). When the bound is above 1 and `Steps::read_ahead()` is above 0, one extra pump thread reads the next items, up to `read_ahead` of them, while the running batch works. For hard jobs it also plans them through `Steps::plan_ahead`. It reads an item only when:
- the item's planned paths are disjoint from every unfinished job of the running batch, using a private copy of the plan's rule (`CHANGELOG.md`, `docs/decisions.md` and `README.md` ignored, unknown paths count as a clash);
- the job limit has room;
- the budget fits a read plus a run;
- every job of the running batch has started, so the pump cannot take money a starting job needs.

A worker that claims an item waits if the pump is mid-read on it, then runs the stored reading with no second read. When a job starts, the paths landed since its reading began are compared with the reading's touched paths. On a hit the drain adds a "stale" note and asks `Steps::recheck`. If that says the reading no longer holds, `Steps::reread` makes a fresh one. The new `Steps` methods all default to off, so `RealSteps` and every existing fake are unchanged, `athena.toml` still has `max_in_flight = 1`, and nothing reads ahead in production yet. With a bound of 1, `drain`, or `read_ahead` 0, no pump thread exists. A CHANGELOG entry and a module-doc bullet describe it. 14 new tests drive `drain_concurrent` and `drain` with a fake `Steps`. They passed in two runs, one single-threaded. Existing tests are untouched; the 15 removed lines are all in production code (`run_job`, `work`, `run_parallel`, the `State` and `Drain` setup).
